skill: Statistics

@ Descriptive Statistics | B
Which measure of central tendency is least affected by extreme outliers?
= Median
- Mean
- Range
- Variance
? The median is the middle value, so a few extreme values barely move it.

@ Descriptive Statistics | B
What is the mean of 2, 4, 6 and 8?
= 5
- 4
- 6
- 20
? The sum is 20 and there are 4 values, so the mean is 5.

@ Descriptive Statistics | I
What is the median of 3, 9, 1, 7 and 10?
= 7
- 6
- 9
- 3
? Sorted values are 1, 3, 7, 9, 10 and the middle value is 7. The mean is 6.

@ Descriptive Statistics | I
What is the mode of 2, 3, 3, 5, 7, 7, 7, 9?
= 7
- 3
- 5
- 9
? The mode is the most frequent value. 7 appears three times.

@ Descriptive Statistics | I
What does the standard deviation measure?
= How spread out the values are around the mean
- The middle value of the data
- The number of observations
- The probability of an event
? A small standard deviation means the values are close to the mean.

@ Probability | I
A fair six-sided die is rolled once. What is the probability of getting a number greater than 4?
= 1/3
- 1/6
- 1/2
- 2/3
? The favorable outcomes are 5 and 6, so the probability is 2/6 = 1/3.

@ Probability | I
Two fair coins are tossed. What is the probability of getting exactly one head?
= 1/2
- 1/4
- 3/4
- 1/3
? The outcomes are HH, HT, TH and TT. Two of the four have exactly one head.

@ Probability | A
If events A and B are independent, what is P(A and B)?
= P(A) x P(B)
- P(A) + P(B)
- P(A) / P(B)
- P(A) - P(B)
? For independent events the probability of both occurring is the product of their probabilities.

@ Distributions | I
Which distribution is symmetric and bell-shaped?
= Normal distribution
- Poisson distribution
- Exponential distribution
- Uniform distribution
? The normal distribution is described by its mean and standard deviation.

@ Distributions | A
In a normal distribution, about what percentage of values fall within one standard deviation of the mean?
= 68%
- 50%
- 95%
- 99.7%
? The empirical rule says roughly 68%, 95% and 99.7% fall within 1, 2 and 3 standard deviations.

@ Inference | A
In hypothesis testing, what usually happens when the p-value is below the chosen significance level, such as 0.05?
= The null hypothesis is rejected
- The null hypothesis is proven true
- The sample is discarded
- The alternative hypothesis is proven with certainty
? A small p-value means the observed data would be unlikely if the null hypothesis were true.

@ Inference | I
What does a correlation coefficient of -0.9 indicate?
= A strong negative linear relationship
- No relationship
- A strong positive linear relationship
- Proof that one variable causes the other
? Values near -1 mean that as one variable rises the other tends to fall.

@ Inference | I
Which statement is correct?
= Correlation alone does not prove causation
- A correlation of 0 proves the variables are independent in every way
- A high correlation proves one variable causes the other
- Causation requires a correlation of exactly 1
? Two variables can move together because of a third factor or by coincidence.

@ Inference | A
Why does the sample variance formula divide by n - 1 instead of n?
= To correct the bias of estimating the population variance from a sample
- To make the calculation faster
- Because one value is always removed as an outlier
- To make the variance negative
? Using n - 1 (Bessel's correction) gives an unbiased estimate of the population variance.
